1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,336
Most crypto wallets that get drained never
leak a seed phrase.

2
00:00:04,336 --> 00:00:08,043
The owner signs the theft themselves, with
one click.

3
00:00:08,043 --> 00:00:13,499
By the end of this video, you'll know
exactly which apps can spend your tokens

4
00:00:13,499 --> 00:00:17,416
right now, and how to cut them off in
about two minutes.

5
00:00:17,416 --> 00:00:18,185
I'm Roland.

6
00:00:18,185 --> 00:00:23,710
I build in DeFi and I've audited smart
contracts, and this is the first check I

7
00:00:23,710 --> 00:00:24,969
run on any wallet.

8
00:00:24,969 --> 00:00:25,739
Quick note:

9
00:00:25,739 --> 00:00:28,536
this is education, not financial advice.

10
00:00:28,536 --> 00:00:33,153
And this video is about Ethereum and the
chains that work like it:

11
00:00:33,153 --> 00:00:36,300
BNB Chain, Base, Arbitrum, Polygon and so
on.

12
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:37,416
Chapter one.

13
00:00:37,416 --> 00:00:39,388
What an approval actually is.

14
00:00:39,388 --> 00:00:44,827
When you swap a token on an exchange like
Uniswap, the exchange's smart contract

15
00:00:44,827 --> 00:00:48,566
needs permission to move that token out of
your wallet.

16
00:00:48,566 --> 00:00:52,509
So before the first swap, your wallet asks
you to approve.

17
00:00:52,509 --> 00:00:55,433
That approval is written on the
blockchain.

18
00:00:55,433 --> 00:00:55,977
It says:

19
00:00:55,977 --> 00:01:00,668
this contract may spend this token from my
wallet, up to this amount.

20
00:01:00,668 --> 00:01:01,960
Here's the problem.

21
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:06,855
To save you fees on future swaps, many
apps ask for an unlimited amount.

22
00:01:06,855 --> 00:01:09,235
And that permission doesn't expire.

23
00:01:09,235 --> 00:01:14,470
It stays live after you close the app,
after you disconnect your wallet, even

24
00:01:14,470 --> 00:01:15,286
years later.

25
00:01:15,286 --> 00:01:20,997
If that contract is ever hacked, or if the
contract was malicious from the start, it

26
00:01:20,997 --> 00:01:23,308
can move every token you approved.

27
00:01:23,308 --> 00:01:24,600
No password needed.

28
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:25,717
Chapter two.

29
00:01:25,717 --> 00:01:28,033
How drainers use this against you.

30
00:01:28,033 --> 00:01:30,417
A wallet drainer is a fake website.

31
00:01:30,417 --> 00:01:36,138
It looks like a free airdrop, a new mint,
a copy of a popular app, or a link someone

32
00:01:36,138 --> 00:01:38,658
replied to you with on X or Telegram.

33
00:01:38,658 --> 00:01:40,292
You connect your wallet.

34
00:01:40,292 --> 00:01:42,676
Then it asks you to sign something.

35
00:01:42,676 --> 00:01:44,515
That signature is the trap.

36
00:01:44,515 --> 00:01:49,623
It might be a normal approve, giving their
contract an unlimited allowance.

37
00:01:49,623 --> 00:01:55,072
For NFTs, it might be set approval for
all, which hands over a whole collection.

38
00:01:55,072 --> 00:01:56,706
Or it might be a permit.

39
00:01:56,706 --> 00:01:59,090
A permit is an off-chain signature.

40
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:04,675
It costs you no gas, it doesn't look like
a transaction, and that's exactly why it

41
00:02:04,675 --> 00:02:05,084
works.

42
00:02:05,084 --> 00:02:10,328
Once they have the signature, they submit
it themselves and move your tokens.

43
00:02:10,328 --> 00:02:11,418
How big is this?

44
00:02:11,418 --> 00:02:16,798
The security firm Scam Sniffer counted
about eighty-four million dollars stolen

45
00:02:16,798 --> 00:02:22,179
by wallet drainer phishing in 2025, from
more than one hundred and six thousand

46
00:02:22,179 --> 00:02:22,724
victims.

47
00:02:22,724 --> 00:02:28,104
The single biggest theft that year was six
and a half million dollars, from one

48
00:02:28,104 --> 00:02:29,262
permit signature.

49
00:02:29,262 --> 00:02:34,643
That's actually down a lot from 2024, when
losses were close to half a billion.

50
00:02:34,643 --> 00:02:36,686
But the method hasn't changed.

51
00:02:36,686 --> 00:02:40,500
It still works because people don't read
what they sign.

52
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:41,761
Chapter three.

53
00:02:41,761 --> 00:02:44,645
Check who can spend your tokens right now.

54
00:02:44,645 --> 00:02:47,186
Open revoke dot cash in your browser.

55
00:02:47,186 --> 00:02:49,726
Type it yourself, don't click a link.

56
00:02:49,726 --> 00:02:53,228
You don't even need to connect your wallet
to look.

57
00:02:53,228 --> 00:02:58,652
Paste your wallet address into the search
bar at the top and pick your network.

58
00:02:58,652 --> 00:02:59,888
You'll see a list.

59
00:02:59,888 --> 00:03:05,656
Each row is an asset, the app or contract
that's allowed to spend it, and the amount

60
00:03:05,656 --> 00:03:07,166
it's allowed to spend.

61
00:03:07,166 --> 00:03:08,677
Look for three things.

62
00:03:08,677 --> 00:03:08,951
One:

63
00:03:08,951 --> 00:03:10,943
anything that says unlimited.

64
00:03:10,943 --> 00:03:11,217
Two:

65
00:03:11,217 --> 00:03:13,414
any spender you don't recognise.

66
00:03:13,414 --> 00:03:13,826
Three:

67
00:03:13,826 --> 00:03:17,603
apps you used once, months ago, and never
went back to.

68
00:03:17,603 --> 00:03:19,251
Prefer a block explorer?

69
00:03:19,251 --> 00:03:24,194
Etherscan has a free Token Approval
Checker that does the same thing for

70
00:03:24,194 --> 00:03:24,812
Ethereum.

71
00:03:24,812 --> 00:03:28,657
BscScan and the other explorers have their
own versions.

72
00:03:28,657 --> 00:03:29,344
Same idea:

73
00:03:29,344 --> 00:03:31,679
paste your address, read the list.

74
00:03:31,679 --> 00:03:34,288
Do this for every network you've used.

75
00:03:34,288 --> 00:03:39,300
An approval on Base doesn't show up on
Ethereum, and the other way round.

76
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:40,457
Chapter four.

77
00:03:40,457 --> 00:03:42,172
Revoke what you don't use.

78
00:03:42,172 --> 00:03:45,403
Now connect the wallet that owns those
approvals.

79
00:03:45,403 --> 00:03:48,634
On revoke dot cash, each row has a revoke
button.

80
00:03:48,634 --> 00:03:52,855
Click it, and your wallet will ask you to
confirm a transaction.

81
00:03:52,855 --> 00:03:56,086
That transaction sets the allowance back
to zero.

82
00:03:56,086 --> 00:04:00,307
Revoking costs a small gas fee, because
it's a real transaction.

83
00:04:00,307 --> 00:04:03,000
On networks like Base or Arbitrum, that's
usually cents.

84
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,550
On Ethereum mainnet it can be more, so
start with the dangerous ones.

85
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:10,067
Here's the order I use.

86
00:04:10,067 --> 00:04:13,825
First, unlimited approvals to anything I
don't recognise.

87
00:04:13,825 --> 00:04:17,255
Second, unlimited approvals to apps I no
longer use.

88
00:04:17,255 --> 00:04:20,816
Third, everything else that's older than a
few months.

89
00:04:20,816 --> 00:04:26,091
Keep the ones you actively use, or revoke
them too and simply approve again next

90
00:04:26,091 --> 00:04:26,421
time.

91
00:04:26,421 --> 00:04:28,004
That's the safest habit.

92
00:04:28,004 --> 00:04:29,322
One important limit.

93
00:04:29,322 --> 00:04:34,796
If you already signed a malicious permit
and the attacker has used it, revoking now

94
00:04:34,796 --> 00:04:36,642
won't bring the tokens back.

95
00:04:36,642 --> 00:04:40,599
Revoking protects you from the next theft,
not the last one.

96
00:04:40,599 --> 00:04:45,677
If you think you've been drained, move
whatever is left to a brand new wallet

97
00:04:45,677 --> 00:04:46,600
straight away.

98
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:47,798
Chapter five.

99
00:04:47,798 --> 00:04:50,078
Read the pop-up before you click.

100
00:04:50,078 --> 00:04:55,260
Most people lose money in the half second
between the pop-up and the click.

101
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:57,678
So slow down and read three things.

102
00:04:57,678 --> 00:04:59,543
What type of request is it?

103
00:04:59,543 --> 00:05:02,030
A connect request alone is harmless.

104
00:05:02,030 --> 00:05:07,350
An approve, set approval for all, permit,
or increase allowance is a spending

105
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:08,110
permission.

106
00:05:08,110 --> 00:05:09,423
Who is the spender?

107
00:05:09,423 --> 00:05:12,117
Your wallet shows the contract address.

108
00:05:12,117 --> 00:05:17,368
If you were trying to mint an NFT and the
request wants permission over your

109
00:05:17,368 --> 00:05:18,612
stablecoins, stop.

110
00:05:18,612 --> 00:05:19,233
How much?

111
00:05:19,233 --> 00:05:22,826
Wallets like MetaMask let you edit the
spending cap.

112
00:05:22,826 --> 00:05:28,008
If you're swapping one hundred dollars,
approve one hundred, not unlimited.

113
00:05:28,008 --> 00:05:31,393
If you can't tell what something does,
reject it.

114
00:05:31,393 --> 00:05:33,189
A real app will ask again.

115
00:05:33,189 --> 00:05:35,400
A scam is hoping you won't look.

116
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:36,520
Chapter six.

117
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:38,501
Habits that make this boring.

118
00:05:38,501 --> 00:05:39,663
Keep two wallets.

119
00:05:39,663 --> 00:05:45,266
A small hot wallet for trying new apps,
airdrops and mints, with only what you can

120
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:46,291
afford to lose.

121
00:05:46,291 --> 00:05:51,825
And a vault wallet, ideally a hardware
wallet, that only ever talks to a few apps

122
00:05:51,825 --> 00:05:52,508
you trust.

123
00:05:52,508 --> 00:05:57,018
Bookmark the apps you use, and only open
them from your bookmarks.

124
00:05:57,018 --> 00:06:02,006
Never connect from a link in a DM, a
reply, or a sponsored search result.

125
00:06:02,006 --> 00:06:04,466
And put a reminder in your calendar:

126
00:06:04,466 --> 00:06:09,180
once a month, open revoke dot cash, paste
your address, and clean up.

127
00:06:09,180 --> 00:06:10,000
Two minutes.

128
00:06:10,300 --> 00:06:12,888
So here's the whole thing in one breath.

129
00:06:12,888 --> 00:06:15,281
Approvals let apps spend your tokens.

130
00:06:15,281 --> 00:06:17,675
Drainers trick you into signing them.

131
00:06:17,675 --> 00:06:22,721
Check your approvals on revoke dot cash,
revoke what you don't use, read every

132
00:06:22,721 --> 00:06:26,020
pop-up, and keep your savings in a
separate wallet.

133
00:06:26,020 --> 00:06:28,932
The full checklist is pinned in the
comments.

134
00:06:28,932 --> 00:06:33,719
In the next video, I'll show you how to
set up a hardware wallet properly,

135
00:06:33,719 --> 00:06:36,824
including the one mistake that makes it
useless.

136
00:06:36,824 --> 00:06:38,894
Education, not financial advice.

137
00:06:38,894 --> 00:06:39,800
See you there.

